{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01NXSNabrkzC6mrQSVtuyYjj", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "The target skill directory contains only `SKILL.md` - no scripts/, no companion files.\n\nNow let me evaluate all 14 patterns against this minimal skill file. The skill is very bare-bones:\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: \"format-code\"\ndescription: \"Format source code files\"\n---\n\n# Format Code\n\nRun the project's code formatter on specified files.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Find the formatter config (`.prettierrc`, `rustfmt.toml`, or `.clang-format`)\n2. Run the formatter on the specified files\n3. Report which files changed\n```\n\nLet me evaluate each pattern:\n\n**1. Activation Metadata (Discovery, Universal)**\n\n\nThe YAML frontmatter exists with a name and description, though the name isn't namespaced and the description is quite generic. No trigger phrases or argument hints are present.\n\n**2. Exclusion Clause (Discovery, Universal)**\nThere's no exclusion clause, no \"Do NOT use\" guidance, and no boundary definition with adjacent skills.\n\n**3. Context Budget (Context Economy, Universal)**\nExamining the content length and complexity to see how much context this skill consumes... token cost and loading only what's needed, this skill trivially satisfies the requirement by being so minimal there's nothing to load\u2014it's lightweight by default rather than through deliberate design. Progressive disclosure doesn't apply since the skill is only 13 lines. For control tuning, the three steps lack distinction between flexible discovery and rigid action phases, with all of them remaining vague and no precise output format specified.\n\nThere's no rationale explaining why each step matters, and while step 3 mentions reporting changed files, there's no template showing what that report should look like. The skill's input-output mapping is fairly straightforward, so examples aren't critical, but the thinking cuts off mid-evaluation. The skill lacks explicit dependencies between steps and has no validation after formatting runs, which could fail silently. There's also no preview or approval mechanism before the formatter modifies user files, and no utility bundle definition yet. I'm going back and forth on whether this skill actually needs helper scripts. The operations are straightforward enough\u2014finding the right formatter and running it\u2014that Claude could handle this without programmatic logic bundled in. I'll mark it as **N/A** since the skill's simplicity means LLM judgment alone is sufficient. report results) are straightforward enough that Claude's built-in tools can handle them without needing a dedicated scripts directory.\n\nFor Autonomy Calibration, the skill lacks any user-invocation controls, model restrictions, or permission gates despite modifying source files, so that's absent too. Counting up the applicable patterns, I'm getting around 12 after excluding the N/A cases.\n\nLooking at what's actually present or strong, I see patterns 1 and 10 showing up clearly. Most others fall into the \"absent but could be improved\" category. Let me double-check pattern 3 on context budget since the skill is only 13 lines and doesn't load external data. The activation test shows this skill would trigger too broadly on any \"format\" + \"code/files\" mention, potentially conflicting with linting or other code operations. The description is sparse with no trigger verbs, exclusion phrases, or argument hints for file paths, making it difficult to distinguish from related operations.\n\nFor Pattern 3, the skill's minimal size and lack of loaded context clearly satisfy the Context Budget requirement. On Pattern 8, while code formatting itself is straightforward (file paths in, formatted files out), the skill doesn't demonstrate how to specify which files or what the output format looks like, so it still lacks the in-skill examples that would make it less ambiguous.\n\nPattern 9 definitely applies since code formatting has well-known edge cases and gotchas. For Pattern 13, I'm deciding whether this skill truly needs helper scripts\u2014it requires finding config files, running formatters, and reporting changes, but these are basic tool operations that Claude can handle without additional programmatic scaffolding, so I'm leaning toward marking it as not requiring a utility bundle.\n\nNow I'm compiling my final assessment across all patterns to see how many are actually present versus absent or not applicable.", "signature": ""}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 211, "cache_read_input_tokens": 49360, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 211, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "274a8245-cc2f-4064-8e97-e316dc33a5c2", "uuid": "dec258c5-a863-41c2-9682-cc51ad85a42b", "request_id": "req_vrtx_011CbcagnwYHRfhtUf7u414r", "timestamp": "2026-06-01T13:01:24.457Z"}
